<h1 class="article-title">Seven cruise ships and more than 4,000 visitors: Liepāja strengthens its position on the Baltic cruise map in 2026</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>During the 2026 cruise season, Liepāja continued to strengthen its position on Baltic Sea cruise itineraries. Seven cruise ships called at the Port of Liepāja this year—the highest number ever welcomed in a single season. These visits not only create memorable experiences for residents and visitors but also make an important economic contribution to local businesses and raise Liepāja’s international profile. Interest from cruise operators remains strong for the next season: eleven calls have already been scheduled for 2027.</p><p>Seven cruise ships brought 4,276 visitors to the Port of Liepāja during the 2026 season. Some passengers used shuttle buses running between the port and city centre every 30 minutes, while others joined organised tours. A total of 86 excursions took place, attended by 2,244 tourists—52.5% of all cruise passengers—demonstrating strong interest in Liepāja’s cultural, historical and tourist attractions.</p><p>Following a lengthy break, cruise ship calls at Liepāja resumed in 2018 as a result of sustained efforts by the Liepāja SEZ Authority and the city to include Liepāja on international cruise routes. According to Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis, Liepāja has already established itself on the Baltic cruise map; the priority now is to build on this achievement and continue developing. Although ambitious, the city aims to welcome more than 20 cruise ships per season in the future, creating new opportunities for local businesses to develop services and experiences for visitors.</p><p>Cruise passengers and crew use local transport, catering, tourism, cultural and entertainment services and visit shops, museums and other attractions. Based on estimates commonly used in the international cruise industry, each passenger spends an average of EUR 70–100 at a destination. This means the total direct economic contribution from this season’s cruise passengers to Liepāja could exceed EUR 400,000.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis emphasises that the value of cruise tourism goes beyond its economic benefits. ‘A cruise ship in port is much more than a vessel at a berth. It gives thousands of people an opportunity to discover Liepāja—its culture, history, business environment and hospitality. Every visitor who leaves with a positive impression becomes an ambassador for our city around the world. At the same time, cruise tourism delivers real economic benefits to local businesses, increases demand for services and helps strengthen Liepāja’s competitiveness in the Baltic Sea region,’ says Hmieļevskis.</p><p>Welcoming cruise ships also plays an important role in promoting the city internationally. Each call gives hundreds or even thousands of people the chance to discover Liepāja at once, helping to establish its image as a modern, welcoming and diverse destination on the Baltic Sea coast.</p><p>One of Liepāja’s advantages is the port’s proximity to the city centre. Within minutes of disembarking, cruise passengers can explore the historic centre, the Great Amber Concert Hall, Karosta, Seaside Park, the beach, museums, cafés and other attractions. Some visitors choose organised tours, while others explore independently.</p><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority, Liepāja Region Tourism Information Office, and the RCA and ESTMA agencies work closely together to attract and welcome cruise ships. Cruise tourism in Liepāja is an investment not only in today’s economy but also in the city’s future, strengthening its appeal as a Baltic Sea destination.</p></div>
